'''walkyr''' (wol′kir), ''n.'' Same as ''valkyr.''

'''walkyrian''' (wol-kir′i-ạn), ''a.'' [< ''walkyrie'' + ''-an.''] Same as ''valkyrian.''

'''walkyrie''' (wol-kir′i), ''n.'' [ME., < AS. ''wælcyrie'' = Icel. ''valkyrja'': see ''valkyr.''] '''1.''' Same as ''valkyr.''—'''2†.''' A wise woman; a fate-reader.
{{smaller block|<poem>As the sage sathrapas that sorsory couthe;
Wychez & ''walkyries'' wonnen to that sale [hall].</poem>
{{right|''Alliterative Poems'' (ed. Morris), ii. 1577.}}}}

'''wall<sup>1</sup>''' (wâl), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wal'', ''walle'', < AS. ''weal'', ''weall'', a rampart of earth, a wall of stone, = OS. ''wal'' = OFries. ''wal'' = D. ''wal'' = MHG. ''wal'', G. ''wall'' = Sw. ''vall'' = Dan. ''vold'', wall, = W. ''gwal'', rampart, < L. ''vallum'', an earthen wall or rampart set with palisades, a row or line of stakes, a wall, rampart, fortification, < ''vallus'', stake, pale, palisade, circumvallation. From the same L. source are ult. E. ''vallate'', ''vallation'', ''circumvallation'', etc. The native AS. word for ‘wall’ is ''wah'': see ''waw''<sup>2</sup>. The L. word for a defensive stone wall is ''murus'': see ''mure''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' A work or structure of stone, brick, or other materials, serving to inclose a space, form a division, support superincumbent weight, or afford a defense, shelter, or security. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') One of the upright inclosing sides of a building or a room.}}
{{smaller block|And the Helynge of here Houses, and the ''Wowes'' and the Dores ben alle of Wode. {{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 247.}}}}
{{smaller block|If the ''walls'' of their [Assyrian palaces’] apartments had not been wainscoted with alabaster slabs, we should never have been able to trace their form with anything like certainty. {{right|''J. Fergusson'', Hist. Arch., I. 161.}}}}
{{smaller block|(''b'') A solid and permanent inclosing fence of masonry, as around a field, a garden, a park, or a town.}}

readings.c3s4:
¶
'''2.''' A rampart; a fortified enceinte or barrier: often in the plural. See cuts under ''chemin-de-ronde'', ''fortification'', and ''retaining wall''.
{{smaller block|<poem>Once more unto the breach, dear friends, once more;
Or close the ''wall'' up with our English dead.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., iii. 1. 2}}}}
